<worksheet component = "cur" xml:id = "wksht-optimization" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Optimization</title>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            <ul>
                <li><title>Dates</title>
                    <p>Assigned Date: November 24, 2026</p>
                    <p>Due Date: December 4, 2026</p>
                </li>
                <li><title>Objective</title>
                    <p>
                        In this assignment, you will work through several scaffolded optimization problems. The steps will guide you through the process of how to structure and solve these problems. The problems to turn in at the end will not have this scaffolding, but you'll want to follow the same process to solve it correctly.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </introduction>

<page>
<paragraphs>
    <title>Scaffolded Problems</title>

<exercise workspace="0.1in">
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Farmer Brown is building a pen for sheep. This pen will be built in a rectangular shape next to a 10 foot long barn, so the part of one side that is adjacent to the barn wall does not need to be fenced. If Farmer Brown has <quantity><mag>90</mag><unit base="foot"/></quantity> of fencing, what are the dimensions of the largest pen (by area) that can be made?
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Draw a picture for this problem. Your sketch should be a rectangle, and part of one side of that rectangle is blocked by a barn. Since we're looking for dimensions in this problem, what should our variables be? Label each side of your diagram with those variables. Make sure to factor in the barn length when you do this.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                What is our objective function? What are we trying to maximize? How can we write that in terms of our variables from the previous part?
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                What constraints do we have on this problem? One of the constraints involves the amount of fencing that Farmer Brown has. The other constraints involve the fact that every length in your picture must be non-negative. Make sure your constraints make every length non-negative, not just some of them.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Use the constraints to convert this problem into a maximization problem for a function of one variable on an interval. You'll need to solve out for one of the variables in one of the constraits, plug that into the objective function, and then determine the interval from the physical constraints. 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Solve the calculus problem. This will involve taking the derivative, setting to zero to find critical points, and then plugging all valid points into the original function. Do we want to consider the critical point here? Is it in the correct interval? Where is the maximum value?
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                State your answer in terms of the original context of the problem. What did the problem ask you for and how can you state that based on the information that you know?
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
</exercise>

</paragraphs>
</page>
<page>

